# Carbon and Its Compounds: The Chemistry of Life

> Carbon's ability to chain with itself — catenation — creates
> millions of compounds. From fuels and fabrics to soaps and food,
> organic chemistry runs daily life.

## 1. Why Carbon Is Special

[definition]
**Catenation** is the self-linking of atoms of one element into
chains and rings; **tetravalency** means carbon forms four bonds.
Together they let carbon build the backbones of fuels, fabrics,
food, and life itself.
[/definition]

## 2. Allotropes of Carbon

| Allotrope | Bonding | Standout property | Use |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Diamond | each C bonded to 4 others; sp³ network | hardest natural substance | cutting, drilling, jewellery |
| Graphite | flat hexagonal layers; sp², one free electron per C | soft, slippery, conducts | pencil leads, lubricants, electrodes |
| Fullerene | C₆₀ "buckyball", 60 C in a football shape | unique cage chemistry | drug delivery, nanotech |
| Graphene | a single graphite layer | strongest material tested | next-generation electronics |

[important]
Graphite conducts because each sp² carbon contributes one
delocalized electron; in diamond every electron is locked into a
σ bond, so it insulates — while its rigid network makes it the
hardest natural substance.
[/important]

## 3. Hydrocarbon Families

@diagram tree id="hydrocarbon-families" layout="tree" aria="Hydrocarbons split into saturated alkanes and unsaturated alkenes and alkynes"
  Hydrocarbons -> "Saturated: alkanes"
  Hydrocarbons -> "Unsaturated"
  "Unsaturated" -> "Alkenes (C=C)"
  "Unsaturated" -> "Alkynes (C triple C)"
@/diagram

| Family | General formula | Bonding | First four members |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Alkanes | CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ | all single | CH₄, C₂H₆, C₃H₈, C₄H₁₀ |
| Alkenes | CₙH₂ₙ | one C=C | C₂H₄, C₃H₆, C₄H₈ |
| Alkynes | CₙH₂ₙ₋₂ | one C≡C | C₂H₂, C₃H₄, C₄H₆ |

A **molecular formula** (C₄H₁₀) only lists atoms; a **structural
formula** shows the arrangement — straight n-butane versus branched
iso-butane.

## 4. Isomerism and Homologous Series

[definition]
**Isomers** share a molecular formula but differ in structure.
Butane (C₄H₁₀) has 2 isomers; pentane (C₅H₁₂) has 3 — n-, iso-, and
neo-.
[/definition]

A **homologous series** is a family sharing one functional group,
each member one CH₂ larger than the last, so melting points climb
smoothly along the series.

## 5. Functional Groups

| Group | Suffix | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Alcohol (–OH) | -ol | ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH |
| Aldehyde (–CHO) | -al | ethanal, CH₃CHO |
| Ketone (–CO–) | -one | propanone, CH₃COCH₃ |
| Carboxylic acid (–COOH) | -oic acid | ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH |
| Alkene (C=C) | -ene | ethene, C₂H₄ |
| Alkyne (C≡C) | -yne | ethyne, C₂H₂ |

## 6. Ethanol and Ethanoic Acid

**Ethanol** (C₂H₅OH) comes from fermenting glucose. "Denatured"
alcohol carries additives that make it unfit to drink; it serves as
a solvent and a fuel. With sodium it releases hydrogen:

@math label="eq:ethanol-sodium"
\mathrm{2C_2H_5OH + 2Na \to 2C_2H_5ONa + H_2}
@/math

**Ethanoic acid** (CH₃COOH) is vinegar's sour agent (about 5%).
The pure acid, freezing near 16.6 °C into ice-like crystals, is
called **glacial**. With ethanol and an acid catalyst it forms the
sweet-smelling **ester**:

@math label="eq:esterification"
\mathrm{CH_3COOH + C_2H_5OH \rightleftharpoons CH_3COOC_2H_5 + H_2O}
@/math

[note]
Esters carry fruity perfumes. Hydrolysing an ester with NaOH makes
soap — **saponification**, the reaction that turned animal fat into
the original soaps.
[/note]

## 7. Soaps and Detergents

[definition]
A **soap** is the sodium or potassium salt of a long-chain fatty
acid. In water its molecules cluster into **micelles**: oily tails
trap grease inside while charged heads face the water.
[/definition]

| | Soap | Detergent |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Works in hard water? | no — Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ form scum | yes |
| Biodegradable? | yes | slower; branched chains persist |
| Made from | fats + NaOH | petrochemicals |

## 8. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Softest useful allotrope? | graphite — also the conductor |
| Alkane general formula? | CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ |
| Isomers of pentane? | 3 |
| Vinegar? | about 5% ethanoic acid |
| Sweet-smelling ester reaction? | esterification |
| Why soap fails in hard water? | Ca/Mg salts form scum |
